---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import Playground from '../islands/Playground.vue'
---
<Layout
  title="Playground — Parsanol"
  description="Edit an input and watch the parsanol wasm engine build the tree, live in your browser."
>
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
  <div class="prose max-w-none mb-8">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Grammar playground</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
      Pick a compiled PARG artifact, edit the input, and watch the wasm engine parse it live.
      Break the input on the <strong>Error recovery</strong> grammar and the engine reports the
      deepest failure position, keeps the partial tree, and — because that grammar declares a
      <code>resync</code> policy — shows the exact span it consumed so parsing could resume.
      Every tree is produced by the same <code>@parsanol/wasm</code> binary that the Rust and
      Ruby conformance suites validate, and the badge reports the artifact's embedded tests
      replayed on that binary.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Playground client:only="vue" />

  <section class="prose max-w-none mt-12 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">What runs where</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Artifacts are checksummed <code>parsanol-tree/v2</code> envelopes compiled ahead of
        time; the browser verifies the checksum before parsing.
      </li>
      <li>
        Parsing, bindings application, the embedded self-test, and error recovery all run in
        the wasm engine (~1.4&nbsp;MB, <code>@parsanol/wasm</code> 0.18.18). No network calls,
        no server-side parsing.
      </li>
      <li>
        A failed parse returns the engine's recovery envelope: the deepest real-content
        position, the expected set, the partial tree parsed before the failure, and — for
        grammars that declare a <code>resync</code> policy — the exact spans consumed so
        parsing could resume.
      </li>
      <li>
        The share button gzips the selected grammar id and input into the URL hash, so a link
        reproduces the playground state without an account or storage.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Author a grammar</strong> compiles PARG text in your browser — the wasm build
      now carries the compiler (rs#298's Rust frontend, shipped in 0.19.2). Compile errors
      and lint rejections surface inline, order-dependence warnings come back with the
      artifact, and the in-file <code>test</code> section replays as the badge. The compiled
      artifact is checksummed and loads through the same verified path as the demos; its
      compiler stamp is the wasm build's version.
    </p>
  </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
